```java
import java.util.*;
import java.util.concurrent.*;

// Lookup by key -> Map (sorted keys -> TreeMap)
Map<String, Product> byId = new HashMap<>();

// Unique tags, insertion order -> LinkedHashSet
Set<String> tags = new LinkedHashSet<>();

// Ordered feed with duplicates, random access -> ArrayList
List<String> feed = new ArrayList<>();

// FIFO queue -> ArrayDeque
Deque<String> jobs = new ArrayDeque<>();

// Shared across threads -> concurrent variant
Map<String, Integer> liveCounts = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
```
Each declaration answers one question: lookup, uniqueness, ordering, access pattern, and concurrency.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class CollectionsDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Integer> nums = new ArrayList<>(List.of(3, 1, 2, 1));

        Collections.sort(nums);                 // [1, 1, 2, 3]
        Collections.reverse(nums);              // [3, 2, 1, 1]
        int max = Collections.max(nums);        // 3
        int freq = Collections.frequency(nums, 1); // 2
        Collections.shuffle(nums);              // random order

        // Descending sort with a comparator:
        Collections.sort(nums, Comparator.reverseOrder());

        // Read-only VIEW (backing list changes still show through):
        List<Integer> view = Collections.unmodifiableList(nums);
        // view.add(9); -> UnsupportedOperationException

        // True immutable snapshot (modern, preferred):
        List<Integer> frozen = List.of(1, 2, 3);
    }
}
```
Each call replaces a hand-written loop, and `List.of` gives a genuinely frozen list, unlike the view from `unmodifiableList`.

```java
class Employee implements Comparable<Employee> {
    String name; int age;
    // Natural order: by name only
    public int compareTo(Employee o) { return name.compareTo(o.name); }
}

List<Employee> emps = new ArrayList<>(...);
Collections.sort(emps);     // by name - fine
// But now I need by age... and by age then name... stuck with one order.
```
A `Comparable` type bakes in a single ordering, so the moment you need a second sort you are stuck.

```java
import java.util.*;

List<Employee> emps = new ArrayList<>(...);

// Sort by age:
emps.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(e -> e.age));

// Sort by age, then by name as a tiebreaker, descending:
emps.sort(Comparator.comparingInt((Employee e) -> e.age)
                    .thenComparing(e -> e.name)
                    .reversed());

// Works even for classes you don't own, via a lambda Comparator.
```
Keep `Comparable` for the single natural order; use `Comparator` for everything situational.
